Ladybird mirrors each vertical-lr line’s inline containing-block
bounds before combining the first and last line corners. This gives
absolute positioning the same line-relative alignment as the visible
inline. The change retains border contraction and the coordinates
used by other writing modes.

Blink’s GatherInlineContainerFragmentsFromItems reads each fragment’s
RectInContainerFragment when gathering inline containing-block
geometry. Gecko’s ComputeInlineAbsoluteCBRect starts with physical
continuation rectangles, converts them into the containing block’s
writing mode, and combines their start and end edges. WebKit’s
PositionedLayoutConstraints::containingBlockOffsetForNonStaticAxes
uses firstFragmentBorderBoxRect and accounts for writing-mode
flips. Each uses positioned fragment geometry, rather than an
unmirrored line-layout offset.

Chrome places the reported host and overlay at x=31. Ladybird places
both at x=30 with its font metrics; the overlay no longer starts
10px to the left. The text-only control and the multi-line and
asymmetric-border controls have the same relative geometry in both
browsers. A separate visible-inline relative-positioning defect
remains outside this change.
